<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sparrenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sparrenburg Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sparrenburg is a castle on a ridge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a>, built by the counts of Ravensberg in the first half of the 13th century. It was the administrative seat of the county and was rebuilt as a fortress with bastions in the 16th century. Its tower and walls, restored in modern times, are a landmark of the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sparrenburg.jpg" alt="Sparrenburg" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bi_Sparrenburg_pano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle stands on the Sparrenberg hill, 180 metres high, 60 metres above the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bielefeld">Bielefeld</a>. It guarded the Bielefeld Pass over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> and protected the town, probably founded around 1200; since castles usually came before towns, an older castle is assumed. It is first mentioned in 1256. When Bernard, Count of Ravensberg, died without heirs in 1346, it passed by marriage to Count Gerhard I of Jülich-Berg and lost its role as a seat of rule, which it held for a last time from 1410 to 1428. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> stayed there overnight on 18 November 1377.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After 1511 the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, now counts of Ravensberg, turned it into a fortress against artillery. A round bastion was added in the west around 1530, and from 1556 the Italian fortress builder Alessandro Pasqualini and his son directed a rebuilding, finished in 1578, that made it the largest fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">War of the Jülich Succession</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> of 1614 gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>. In the Thirty Years' War Spanish troops took it in 1623, and Swedes and Hessians besieged them for nearly a year before it was handed over in 1637. It withstood its last sieges in 1673 and 1679, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the end of the 17th century the fortress no longer met military needs and was partly used as a prison; its outer walls were later pulled down with the agreement of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a>. A citizens' committee rebuilt the tower from 1842, the city bought the site from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1879, and a Gothic hall with a ballroom, restaurant and museum rooms opened on 24 April 1888. Used as a flak emplacement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, it was heavily damaged in the air raid of 30 September 1944, which only the tower survived, and was restored between 1948 and 1987.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sparrenberg Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their territory grew between the Teutoburg Forest and the Weser hills, and around 1214 Count Hermann IV founded the town of Bielefeld beneath the <b>Sparrenburg</b> castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a></li></ul></section>
